<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 6</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 6 Walkthrough: Guest Bedroom</h1>

    <p>Guest Bedroom is the sixth room of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and the quietest one so far: a single bedroom, no corridor, no second floor. That calm is misleading. The room hides a two-dial padlock that most players walk straight past, a chest with five arrow slots, a phone that wants a name rather than a number, and a clock that only becomes a puzzle after the lights go out. This page gives you the two answers that matter — <b>94</b> and <b>DAVID</b> — and says plainly which of the remaining puzzles we could not read off the footage.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a single 6:25 recording, sampled at one frame per second, with the hamper dial re-sampled at four frames per second around the moment it opens. <b>94</b> was read from the dial's centre row twice, on two separate crops of the same second, and crosses to the lamp's <b>IX</b> and <b>IV</b>. <b>DAVID</b> was read off the ID card at 1:50 and matched to the phone's five-character password field at 2:15. No number or word on this page is copied from another guide.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={6} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Guest Bedroom is room 6 of 15 in Chapter 3. The numeric lock takes <b>94</b>; the phone takes the name <b>DAVID</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Look at the table lamp beside the bed. Two Roman numerals are printed on the shade: <b>IX</b> and <b>IV</b> — 9 and 4.</li>
        <li>Turn the two dials of the padlock on the grey quilted hamper so the centre row reads <b>9</b> then <b>4</b>. The hamper opens.</li>
        <li>Open the dark chest under the gallery of nine framed pictures, take the <b>ID card</b> and read the name on it: <b>DAVID</b>.</li>
        <li>Tap the phone on the console and type <b>DAVID</b> into the text bar, then <b>Done</b>. The red padlock turns green.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="mvjKFb0bhrs" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 6 Guest Bedroom Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 6 Guest Bedroom Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mvjKFb0bhrs/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 6 Guest Bedroom walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/mvjKFb0bhrs/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 6:25 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-hero-guest-bedroom.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 6 Guest Bedroom: a cutaway view of the room with the bed against the back wall, two nightstands with lamps, a console of vinyl records and a turntable on the left, a floral rug and a dark chest" width="1200" height="675" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The camera opens on a cutaway of the whole room, which is the best look you get at it: a bed against the back wall with a beige headboard, a light duvet and a teal throw; two matching nightstands, each with a lamp; a large floral rug in grey and pale green; and on the left a long wooden console loaded with vinyl records, with a turntable, a speaker and a globe on top. Above that console a gallery of <b>nine framed pictures</b> hangs in a 3x3 grid, and beneath it, on the floor, sits a dark wooden chest with metal banding.</p>
    <p>Against the left wall there is a white two-door cabinet; open it and there is a cushion with a gold anchor on it. The mirror above it is round and dark-framed. In the foreground, near the camera, a grey quilted hamper — that is the object with the padlock, and it is the one thing in the room that is easy to crop out of your view while you hunt for a code.</p>
    <p>The level runs on four mechanisms: the <b>padlock</b> (two dials, answered by the lamp), the <b>clock</b> (a set-the-hands puzzle once the lights drop), the <b>chest</b> (five arrow slots, answered by the picture gallery, which yields the ID card), and the <b>phone</b> (a text password, answered by the card). The safe with the fruit keypad is the fifth and last lock, and it is the one we could not finish reading.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Every interactive object we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Table lamp</td><td>Left nightstand, beside the bed</td><td>Two Roman numerals on the shade: <b>IX</b> and <b>IV</b> — the source of 94.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grey quilted hamper</td><td>Foreground, near the camera</td><td>Carries the room's two-dial padlock; opens for a hammer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wall clock</td><td>Floats over the bed once the room dims</td><td>Set-the-hands puzzle at 0:36; the only lit object while the lights are down.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Console and vinyl shelf</td><td>Left wall, under the gallery</td><td>Turntable, speaker, globe and a stack of records; the phone lies here.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Globe</td><td>On top of the console</td><td>Lifts to show a pattern of markings; examined closely at 5:13 and 6:03.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nine framed pictures</td><td>Left wall, 3x3 gallery above the chest</td><td>Each frame carries a small grid-and-arrow pattern — the clue for the chest's five slots.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dark wooden chest</td><td>Floor, under the gallery</td><td>Five gold-framed arrow slots; opens to the <b>DAVID</b> ID card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Smartphone</td><td>On the console</td><td>Red padlock screen and a text bar; takes <b>DAVID</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Black safe</td><td>Right side of the room</td><td>Circular dial plus a 3x3 fruit-and-vegetable keypad and a six-slot display.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White two-door cabinet</td><td>Left wall, under the round mirror</td><td>Holds a cushion with a gold anchor on it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Door</td><td>Left wall, beside the cabinet</td><td>Opens with the assembled Key, not a keypad — the exit.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:02</span>
      <strong>Read the room from the opening cutaway</strong>
      The first two seconds are the only wide shot you get. Everything you need is in it: the bed and two nightstands at the back, the console with the records and globe on the left, the nine-picture gallery, the chest beneath it, the white cabinet, and the hamper in the foreground. Note where the hamper is now, because later the camera spends a full minute zoomed into the padlock on it and you will want to know which object you are looking at.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-hero-guest-bedroom.jpg" alt="The Guest Bedroom at the start of Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 6, showing the bed, nightstands, record console, gallery wall and chest" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=2s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:02</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:06 · the lamp</span>
      <strong>IX and IV are printed on the shade</strong>
      Zoom on the lamp on the left nightstand and two Roman numerals are visible on the fabric of the shade, one above the other: <b>IX</b> and <b>IV</b>. They are not decoration and they are not the date — they are the only two values in the room that come in a pair, and the only lock in the room with two dials is the one on the hamper. <b>IX</b> is 9. <b>IV</b> is 4.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-lamp-roman-numerals.jpg" alt="The bedside lamp in Guest Bedroom with the Roman numerals IX and IV printed on its shade" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=6s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:12 · the padlock</span>
      <strong>Two dials, centre row, 9 then 4</strong>
      The padlock zooms in and it is smaller than it looks: <b>two</b> dial columns, each showing three digits at a time in a vertical strip. The top and bottom digits are only there so you can see what is coming; the <b>middle row</b> is the setting. Left column: 0 above, <b>9</b> in the middle, 8 below. Right column: 5 above, <b>4</b> in the middle, 3 below. That is the whole code — <b>9 4</b>. Anyone who reads the top row instead gets 05 and wonders why nothing happens.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-hamper-padlock-94.jpg" alt="The two-dial padlock on the grey quilted hamper in Guest Bedroom, set with 9 on the left dial and 4 on the right" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=12s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:19 · inside the hamper</span>
      <strong>The lid comes off and there is a hammer</strong>
      Once the dials sit on 9 and 4 the zoom closes, the hamper's lid lifts and a <b>hammer</b> is lying inside on the quilted fabric. Take it. The recording does not show the hammer being used in the same shot, but it is the only tool the room hands you this early, and the chest's lock is the next thing the player attacks, so keep it in the bag rather than assuming it is scenery.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=19s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:36 · lights out</span>
      <strong>The wall clock is a set-the-hands puzzle</strong>
      At 0:36 the room drops into darkness and a large white analogue clock appears over the bed — black numerals, black hands, and nothing else lit. This is not a number you carry to a keypad: the clock itself is the mechanism, and you turn its hands. The recording solves it inside the blackout. We are not printing a target time for it, because different frames of the same dial read differently and a wrong time would send you round in circles.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-wall-clock.jpg" alt="The large white wall clock that appears over the bed in Guest Bedroom when the room goes dark" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=36s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:30 · the chest</span>
      <strong>Five slots, five arrows, one clue wall</strong>
      The dark chest under the gallery is fastened by a strip of <b>five square slots</b> in gold frames, each holding an arrow you rotate. The clue is directly above it: the <b>nine framed pictures</b> in their 3x3 grid, every frame holding a small grid-and-arrow pattern. Work the gallery, then set the five arrows. In the recording the chest gives at <b>1:50</b>; we describe the mechanism rather than an order because the arrow sequence changes faster than one frame per second can carry.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=90s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:50 · the ID card</span>
      <strong>The card says DAVID</strong>
      The chest opens onto a white card with a teal wave design. In large black capitals it reads <b>DAVID</b>, with an ID number below, a join date of <b>07/21</b>, a valid-thru date of <b>09/22</b> and a QR code in the corner. The name is the point: it is a five-letter word, and the only five-letter password in this room belongs to the phone. Do not overthink the ID number — the game does not ask for it anywhere in this level.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-id-card-david.jpg" alt="The white ID card found in the chest in Guest Bedroom, printed with the name DAVID in black capitals" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=110s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:50</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 2:12 · the phone</span>
      <strong>Type DAVID into the text bar</strong>
      The phone shows a <b>red padlock</b> with a keyhole, a line of placeholder dashes beneath it and the game's white text bar at the bottom of the screen with <b>Done</b> and <b>Cancel</b>. This is the one lock in the room that wants letters. Type <b>DAVID</b> and press <b>Done</b>; three seconds later the lock is <b>green</b> and the screen shows a row of masked characters — the game confirms the password without ever displaying it back to you in plain text.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=132s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:18 · the Phone paper</span>
      <strong>A 3x3 field of dots with shapes around them</strong>
      A grey square labelled <b>Phone</b> appears with a grid of dots and small geometric shapes — squares, triangles and circles — arranged around them. This is the clue sheet for the phone's pattern layer, which the game uses on top of the text password. It is a picture of a pattern, not a word, and one frame per second is not enough to transcribe it faithfully; the sheet is included here so you know what it looks like when you meet it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-phone-pattern-paper.jpg" alt="The grey Phone clue sheet in Guest Bedroom showing a 3x3 grid of dots with squares, triangles and circles around them" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=138s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:18</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 3:24 · the safe</span>
      <strong>Fruit on the keys, six slots on the display</strong>
      The black safe has a circular dial on the door and, below it, a <b>3x3 keypad of fruit and vegetable symbols</b> — no digits anywhere. Above the keypad sits a display of <b>six slots</b>, which tells you the code is six symbols long and drawn from those nine. The recording works the safe towards the end of the run. This is the one mechanism in Guest Bedroom we could not read to the end, and it is listed as unverified below.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-safe-symbol-keypad.jpg" alt="The black safe in Guest Bedroom with a circular dial and a 3x3 keypad of fruit and vegetable symbols under a six-slot display" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=204s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 5:13 · the globe</span>
      <strong>Two close-ups of the globe's markings</strong>
      The camera returns to the console for two long close-ups of the <b>globe</b>, framed by white corner brackets. In the first the sphere is covered in small dark marks; in the second the same surface reads as a grid. Something about the markings changes between the two views, which is typical of a rotate-the-globe puzzle, and it is the last piece of room business before the safe and the door.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-globe-desk.jpg" alt="The globe, speaker and bowl on the console in Guest Bedroom, framed by white corner brackets" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=313s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:13</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 6:24 · out</span>
      <strong>Two key parts become the Key</strong>
      The end of the level does not use a keypad. The inventory holds two separate <b>Key part</b> items, using one on the other produces a single <b>Key</b>, and that key opens the door on the left wall. At <b>6:24</b> the doorway stands open with the black interior behind it and the white <b>exit icon</b> glowing in the frame. That is the last gameplay shot before the video ends.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l6-exit-door.jpg" alt="The Guest Bedroom door standing open with the white exit icon in the doorway at the end of Chapter 3 Level 6" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvjKFb0bhrs&t=384s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>The padlock looks like it has three dials.</b> It has two. Each dial shows the digit above, the setting and the digit below in one strip, and on a phone screen the three bands read like three separate wheels. Count the columns, not the numbers: two columns, middle row, <b>9</b> and <b>4</b>.</p>
    <p><b>Nobody looks at the lamp.</b> The IX and IV are small, they are on the fabric rather than the base, and the lamp is one of a matching pair, which makes it look like set dressing. If you are hunting a two-digit code with no idea where to start, the lamp is the answer and the shade is where it is written.</p>
    <p><b>The hamper is cropped out of the frame.</b> It sits in the foreground, below the camera's usual eye line, and the zoom into the padlock does not show you where it is in the room. If you have 94 and cannot find the lock, look down and forward, not at the furniture along the walls.</p>
    <p><b>The clock gives no number.</b> When the lights drop and the clock appears, players wait for a code to pop up. It never does — the clock is the lock. Move the hands on the clock face itself.</p>
    <p><b>The chest's arrows seem random.</b> They are not: the nine framed pictures above the chest each carry a small grid-and-arrow pattern, and the chest has five slots. Work the gallery first, in reading order, and treat the frames as a sequence rather than as decoration.</p>
    <p><b>The phone rejects every number you try.</b> It is not a numeric lock and it is not asking for the ID number on the card. It wants the name printed at the top of the card, five letters, typed into the white bar and confirmed with <b>Done</b>.</p>

    <h2>Where Guest Bedroom sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Guest Bedroom is room <b>6 of 15</b>: after Workshop (5) and before Master Bathroom (7). It is the chapter's first bedroom and its first level built around a piece of furniture you have to walk to rather than a wall you can look at — the chest, the console and the hamper are all over the floor, and the camera makes you cross the room to use them.</p>
    <p>Its lock design is also a step up. Workshop and Dining Room both hand you a code and a keypad. Guest Bedroom splits the work: <b>94</b> comes from two Roman numerals on a lamp, which you have to convert, and <b>DAVID</b> comes from a card you have to find before the phone will even accept input. That two-stage chain — find the card, then use the card — is the pattern Master Bathroom reuses with its three numbered tiles.</p>

