    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, separated from the verified answers above so you know exactly what you are relying on.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The clock's target time.</b> The puzzle is real and the clock is the mechanism, but the dial is read at an angle and different frames of the same second give different readings. No time is printed here. If you solve it, the answer is worth writing down: it is one of the few things in this room that the recording does not give away.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The chest's five-arrow order.</b> We can see the five slots and the nine framed pictures clearly, and the chest demonstrably opens at 1:50. The order the arrows are set in changes inside a single second, so any sequence printed here would be a guess dressed up as a fact.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The safe's six-symbol code.</b> The fruit-and-vegetable keypad and the six-slot display are both legible at 3:24, but the six symbols the safe accepts are never held long enough to read, and the safe's interior is not shown in a clean frame. This is the one lock in Guest Bedroom we cannot give a code for.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. DAVID as the literal password string.</b> The card is unambiguous — the name is printed in large capitals at 1:50 — and the phone accepts a masked five-character password at 2:15, which matches the name exactly. The characters themselves are masked on screen, so this is an identification of the card's name rather than a read of the typed string. If the phone refuses DAVID, try the name in lower case before assuming the page is wrong.
    </div>
